  "verified": {
    "opened_data_files": true,
    "what_i_actually_checked": "Downloaded and opened from the GitHub repository: database_clean.csv (27,010 rows x 55 columns, 36 MB unzipped) - counted non-null trait values (Gram stain 9,990; oxygen tolerance 10,874; motility 9,331; spore formation 4,854; FAPROTAX metabolism 17,283; Fermentation Explorer type 8,194); gene_functions_database.rds (22,734,429 genome-KEGG-ortholog pairs, 15,100 unique genomes, 11,866 unique KEGG orthology IDs, zero duplicate pairs, median 1,474 orthologs per genome); 16S_ribosomal_sequences.fasta (21,119 sequences, median 1,465 nucleotides, every header matches an LPSN ID in the main table); three raw BacDive exports (gram_stain.csv 10,383 rows, oxygen_tolerance.csv 13,719, motility.csv 9,970 - none has a column flagging predicted vs measured values). Verified the joins: 21,119 organisms carry a 16S sequence, 15,100 carry gene content, 14,903 carry both; 9,806 have an unambiguous positive/negative Gram stain plus a 16S sequence (38.5% positive), spread over 477 families of which 101 have at least 20 members. Also opened the supplement (Supplementary Datasets S1-S4 in one xlsx): S1 = 100 model organisms with trait columns, S3 = 215 rows of per-trait balanced accuracy / sensitivity / specificity for the three tools, S4 = 22 rows comparing against FAPROTAX, DRAM and BacDive-AI. Checked the Zenodo record (CC-BY-4.0, one 167 MB zip) and the GitHub file tree (31 pre-trained random-forest .rds model files).",
    "what_came_from_paper_text_only": "The reported headline accuracies (balanced accuracy averaging 0.81 taxonomy / 0.86 metabolic networks / 0.88 machine learning at species rank), the claim that performance falls at family rank and above (Supplementary Figure S5, inside an 8.3 MB PDF I did not text-extract), the provenance of each source database, and the statement that the machine-learning tool follows MICROPHERRET's approach. I did not run the web server or any of the three tools."
  },
  "part_a_killers": {
    "a1_downloadable": {"pass": true, "note": "Every file fetched anonymously from raw.githubusercontent.com; no login, no application. Zenodo mirror is CC-BY-4.0. Total needed for the project is about 82 MB, all opened successfully."},
    "a2_label_is_a_column": {"pass": true, "note": "Traits are literal columns in database_clean.csv ('Gram stain (BacDive)' = positive/negative/variable; 'Oxygen tolerance (BacDive)'; 'Motility (BacDive)'; 'Type of metabolism (Fermentation Explorer)' = Fermentation/Other/Methanogenesis). Multi-source entries are semicolon-joined strings needing a parsing rule, not construction: 9,806 of 9,977 Gram labels are already a single unambiguous word."},
    "a3_inputs_present": {"pass": true, "note": "Inputs are actual data, not accessions: 21,119 full-length 16S rRNA sequences in a FASTA keyed by LPSN ID, and 22.7 million (genome, KEGG ortholog) presence pairs keyed by IMG genome ID, which is itself a column in the main table. 15,100 of 15,101 genome IDs resolve into the gene table."},
    "a4_units_stated": {"pass": true, "note": "Labels used here are categorical with a controlled vocabulary (positive/negative, aerobe/anaerobe). Continuous columns carry units in the name ('Cell length in microns (BacDive)', 'Temperature for growth in degrees (BacDive)', 'Salt for growth in moles per liter'). Verified in the header."},
    "a5_enough_rows": {"pass": true, "n_rows": 9806, "note": "9,806 organisms with an unambiguous Gram stain and a 16S sequence (verified by counting); 10,855 with oxygen tolerance, 9,318 motility, 7,892 fermentation yes/no. With gene content instead of 16S: 7,464 / 8,081 / 7,009. Far above the 400-row classification floor."},
    "a6_fits_laptop": {"pass": true, "working_set": "~0.5 GB", "note": "82 MB downloaded; the largest object is the gene-ortholog table at 184 MB in memory in R, and the sparse genome x ortholog matrix is 22.7M non-zeros. DNA language-model embeddings for 21,119 sequences fit in ~65 MB. No GPU required."},
    "a7_counts_match_paper": {"pass": true, "note": "Supplementary Dataset S1 holds exactly 100 model organisms as the paper states, and the three-tool comparison in S4 matches Figure 3B. One drift: the paper says 'one of 30 pre-trained models' while the current repository has 31 random-forest files, because the repository (updated 2026-09-10) has moved past the published version. Use the Zenodo v1.0.4 snapshot for a frozen count."},
    "a8_label_provenance": {"pass": true, "note": "Two distinct provenances, and the distinction decides the project. BacDive traits are curated laboratory observations on culture-collection strains, which is what we want - BUT BacDive has since merged its own random-forest predictions into strain records (41,128 of them, June 2026), and this export is dated 2024-11-07 with no column flagging predicted vs measured, so a spot-check against BacDive is mandatory before trusting them. Separately, the 'Type of metabolism (FAPROTAX)' columns are assigned by taxon-name lookup, so predicting them from 16S sequence or taxonomy would be circular; these columns must be excluded from the proposed task."
  },
