  "controls": {
    "positive_control": "Predict phylum from the 16S embedding - must be near-perfect, since 16S is the standard phylogenetic marker; and Gram-positive calls must concentrate in Bacillota and Actinomycetota, spore formation in Bacillaceae and Clostridia.",
    "negative_control": "Shuffle trait labels globally and again within family (performance must fall to the per-family majority); replace the embedding with random vectors of the same shape; fit on 6-mer counts alone to see how much the language model actually adds.",
    "external_validation": "The paper's 100 literature-curated model organisms (Supplementary Dataset S1, opened and counted) and the authors' own lab-measured rumen isolates (Supplementary Dataset S4), plus the independent Madin et al. trait compilation joined on NCBI taxonomy ID."
  },
  "honest_claim_h3": "For a bacterium or archaeon known only by its full-length 16S ribosomal RNA gene, and belonging to a family with no cultured, trait-characterised member, the model calls Gram stain, oxygen tolerance and fermentative ability with balanced accuracy X - a claim that holds only for validly published, cultured type strains, the sole organisms for which curated trait records exist.",
  "plain_idea": {
    "one_liner": "Use a microbe's 16S ribosomal RNA gene sequence to predict what it does: oxygen tolerance, fermentation, Gram stain, motility.",
    "train_on": "Full-length 16S ribosomal RNA gene sequences for 21,119 named, cultured bacteria and archaea (counted in the FASTA file), each linked to curated trait records; 9,806 of them have an unambiguous Gram stain and 10,855 an oxygen-tolerance value. For the subset with a sequenced genome (14,903), the presence or absence of 11,866 KEGG orthology gene families is available as an alternative input.",
    "predict": "A yes/no call per trait with a probability. The true answer comes from BacDive, a curated database of laboratory observations on culture-collection strains (Gram stain, oxygen tolerance, motility, spore formation), and from literature-curated fermentation records assembled by the same authors.",
    "why_worthwhile": "Nearly all microbiome surveys produce only 16S sequences, and most of those sequences belong to organisms with no cultured relative; measuring how fast trait prediction decays with evolutionary distance tells ecologists exactly when the trait profiles produced by FAPROTAX, PICRUSt2 and Microbe Decoder stop being meaningful."
  },
  "paper_type": "computational",
  "crowded_field_case": {
    "more_or_better_data": {
      "answer": "partly",
      "why": "Our labelled set (9,806 Gram, 10,855 oxygen tolerance) is not bigger than BacDive-AI's 15,938 strains or MICROPHERRET's 14,364 genomes. But none of them trains on 16S rRNA sequence, so for that input there is no competing training set at all, and this repository is the only place where sequence, gene content and three independent trait sources sit side by side for the same 27,010 organisms."
    },
    "confidence_score": {
      "answer": "partly",
      "why": "Existing tools already emit a forest-vote probability, and BacDive only imports predictions above 90% confidence - so raw confidence is not new. What is new: with 101 families of at least 20 members we can calibrate confidence as a function of distance to the nearest training relative, and 56 internally contradictory Gram entries plus cross-database disagreement give a label-noise floor none of them report."
    },
    "models_beatable": {
      "answer": "partly",
      "why": "MICROPHERRET (2024) and BacDive-AI (2025) are random forests and SVMs on presence/absence features evaluated with stratified random cross-validation, which leaks phylogeny and is beatable on out-of-family tests. This paper's own tool (2026) was already evaluated rank-by-rank, so it is honest within genus and hard to beat there."
    },
    "guaranteed_improvement": {
      "answer": "no",
      "why": "The 16S gene carries strictly less information than the genome, so a 16S model does not see everything the gene-content baselines see - the key condition fails. We can run their baselines on the same split, but improvement is a genuine empirical question, not a construction."
    }
  },
