  "pitch": {
    "headline": "27,010 cultured prokaryotes with curated traits, 21,119 full-length 16S sequences and 22.7 million genome-gene links, all downloadable and all opened and counted here.",
    "what_makes_the_data_unusual": "Three inputs for the same organisms - name, 16S sequence, KEGG ortholog content - with traits from three independent curation efforts, and 477 families (101 with 20+ members) for phylogeny-aware splits. 82 MB, no login.",
    "the_win": "A 16S-only model at 0.75 or better balanced accuracy for Gram stain on families with no trait-characterised relative, against 0.5 for majority-class guessing and BacDive-AI's 0.95 F1 obtained on a phylogenetically leaky random split.",
    "even_if_it_fails": "We get a measured curve of trait-prediction accuracy against 16S distance, which tells microbiome ecologists the distance at which FAPROTAX- and PICRUSt2-style trait profiles stop meaning anything.",
    "who_wants_it": "The Hackmann lab at UC Davis who built this server, the IMG team at the Joint Genome Institute (a co-author), and anyone deciding whether to report trait profiles for unnamed amplicon sequences."
  },
  "ml_idea": "Build one joined table from the Microbe Decoder repository (LPSN ID links organism to its 16S rRNA sequence; IMG genome ID links it to KEGG ortholog presence), then predict four laboratory-measured traits - Gram stain, oxygen tolerance, motility, spore formation - plus literature-curated fermentative ability, under leave-whole-family-out and 16S-identity-clustered splits. Compare three input types head to head: (1) taxonomy nearest-relative lookup, the FAPROTAX/Microbe Decoder approach; (2) genome gene content with a random forest, the MICROPHERRET/BacDive-AI approach, runnable as a published baseline; (3) a frozen DNA language model embedding of the full-length 16S gene with a small head, which nobody appears to have tried for organism traits. The deliverable is a calibrated accuracy-versus-evolutionary-distance curve per trait, plus a reusable benchmark with the splits. Exclude the FAPROTAX trait columns from the sequence task because they are taxon-name lookups and would be circular.",
  "effort": {
    "hours_to_feature_table": 6,
    "working_set_gb": 0.5,
    "needs_gpu": false,
    "peak_ram_gb": 3,
    "embedding_hours": 10,
    "cv_run_minutes": 15,
    "compute_note": "Estimated from file sizes and row counts I measured: the 22.7-million-row gene table loads in about a minute and occupies 184 MB in R; the 16S FASTA is 31 MB. Embedding time (21,119 sequences of ~1,465 nucleotides through a small DNA language model on CPU) is an estimate, not timed.",
    "cheapest_killing_check": "Count how many Gram-stain and oxygen-tolerance values in the 2024-11-07 BacDive export are BacDive's own machine-learning predictions rather than measurements - pull 50 of those strains from BacDive and look for the AI flag. If a large share are predicted, the labels are another model's output; fall back to the 8,194 literature-curated Fermentation Explorer entries."
  },
  "verdict": "verify_first",
  "verdict_reason": "Everything in Part A checks out in the files I opened: 27,010 organisms, 21,119 full-length 16S sequences, 22.7 million genome-gene pairs, 9,806 clean Gram labels over 477 families, all free and under 100 MB, with published per-trait accuracies to reproduce. The project - predicting laboratory-measured traits from the 16S gene with a frozen DNA language model, scored on held-out families - is one nobody appears to have done, and it cannot produce a useless answer because the decay curve is the result. Two things must be checked before starting, and both are cheap: first, whether the November-2024 BacDive export silently contains BacDive's own random-forest predictions, which would make the labels another model's output; second, the project must drop the FAPROTAX trait columns, since those are taxon-name lookups and predicting them from sequence would be circular by construction."
}
